        {
          "entity": {
            "entity_id": "01a16114-6c12-7238-bbd7-48d0df889171",
            "entity_type": "panel",
            "name": "StackArmor Method",
            "dimensions": {
              "tap.graph": "web"
            }
          },
          "node": {
            "name": "StackArmor Method",
            "slug": "playground-stackarmor-intro",
            "description": "The page title and the method in one paragraph.",
            "view": "tap_web/panels/text_panel.html",
            "config": {
              "hide_header": false,
              "text": "FedRAMP publishes the PAIN levels N1 to N5 and the remediation matrix, and not a rule that turns a vulnerability on an asset into a level. stackArmor's memo supplies one: re-weight the CVSS impact of the vulnerability by what the asset it sits on requires, rescale, name the result, then turn the name into an N-level with the affected-agency flag. Everything below is computed live from this grid; nothing is stored."
            },
            "editor_view": "tap_web/panels/text_panel_editor.html"
          }
        },
        {
          "entity": {
            "entity_id": "01a16114-6c12-7238-bbd7-48d1b4f40854",
            "entity_type": "panel",
            "name": "The Method",
            "dimensions": {
              "tap.graph": "web"
            }
          },
          "node": {
            "name": "The Method",
            "slug": "playground-stackarmor-method",
            "description": "The formula and the thresholds.",
            "view": "tap_web/panels/text_panel.html",
            "config": {
              "hide_header": false,
              "text": "Impact C, I, A (CVSS): None 0, Low 0.22, High 0.56.   Requirement CR, IR, AR (the asset): Low 0.5, Medium 1.0, High 1.5.\nU = 1 - (1 - C x CR)(1 - I x IR)(1 - A x AR).   D_H = 0.995904 (the largest U possible).   S = U / D_H.\nWord: Minimal below U 0.28; Narrow below U 0.56; Disruptive below S 0.933; Debilitating from there.\nN-level: Minimal N1; Narrow N2; Disruptive N3 for one agency, N4 for several; Debilitating N4 for one agency, N5 for several.\nNothing is lowered without evidence: a missing CVSS vector counts as High, an unstamped program as High on all three requirements, and the affected-agency flag defaults to several agencies."
            },
            "editor_view": "tap_web/panels/text_panel_editor.html"
          }
        },
        {
          "entity": {
            "entity_id": "01a16114-6c12-7238-bbd7-48d21a4973e2",
            "entity_type": "panel",
            "name": "From Finding To Deadline",
            "dimensions": {
              "tap.graph": "web"
            }
          },
          "node": {
            "name": "From Finding To Deadline",
            "slug": "playground-stackarmor-steps",
            "description": "The steps, and what does each on the system.",
            "view": "tap_web/panels/text_panel.html",
            "config": {
              "hide_header": false,
              "text": "1. A scanner finding concerns a vulnerability (CONCERNS_COMPLIANCE_VULNERABILITY) and is carried by a component (CARRIES_COMPLIANCE_FINDING).\n2. The owner: the base reach (fedramp_20x_vdr) names the program that owns the component in a container, or the container itself.\n3. Impact: C, I and A come from the vulnerability's CVSS vector; no vector means High.\n4. Requirements: the owner program's stamp (asset_profile, PROFILES_PROGRAM) gives CR, IR and AR, from an archetype (USES_ARCHETYPE) or its own values.\n5. Score: U and S, then the word, then the N-level with the affected-agency flag (stamped, or the Search input).\n6. Exposure: likely exploitable (KEV, EPSS, or evidence) and internet-reachable choose the remediation column.\n7. Deadline: the FedRAMP timeframe row for the class, the level and the column, in hours.\nRun by the Searches stackarmor-pain, stackarmor-summary and stackarmor-trace; computed on read."
            },
            "editor_view": "tap_web/panels/text_panel_editor.html"
          }
        },
